    page: 9
HIT:
    index: 2640
    score: 0.3
    filename: DBLP_conf_icml_PuMSK18.pdf
    page: 9
answer=The documents describe sketching as a way to combine human guidance with automated synthesis. In the paper’s words, it is “a new form of localized software synthesis” where “the programmer uses partial programs to describe the desired implementation strategy, and leaves the low-level details of the implementation to an automated synthesis procedure.”

Compared with fully handwritten programming, sketching reduces the amount of detail the programmer must specify: “By combining the programmers insight expressed in the sketch with the synthesizer’s ability to reason exhaustively about all the low-level details of the problem, sketching allows complex algorithms to be implemented with minimal human effort.”

Compared with other program-generation techniques, the provided documents do not give a detailed side-by-side comparison. They only indicate that sketching is related to “program synthesis” and “program sketching,” and cite prior work such as “Combinatorial sketching for finite programs” and “Program sketching.” So, from these documents alone, I can say sketching is a synthesis approach that uses partial programs and automated completion, but I can’t reliably compare it to specific other techniques beyond that.
